---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import Header from '../../components/Header.astro';
import Footer from '../../components/Footer.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../../components/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import ProductSubNav from '../../components/ProductSubNav.astro';
import DisclosureBoundary from '../../components/research/DisclosureBoundary.astro';
import { RESEARCH_ROUTES } from '../../data/research';
import { COMPANY_NAME } from '../../data/company';

const SUBNAV = [
  { label: 'Research Overview', href: RESEARCH_ROUTES.landing },
  { label: 'Research Thesis', href: RESEARCH_ROUTES.thesis },
  { label: 'Plain English', href: RESEARCH_ROUTES.plainEnglish },
  { label: 'Architecture', href: '/architecture' },
  { label: 'Current Status', href: '/progress' },
  { label: 'Validation Evidence', href: '/progress/validation-evidence' },
];

// The four public names a nontechnical reader will actually encounter, described in one line
// each. Kept here rather than inline so the ordering and the one-liners stay together.
const PIECES = [
  {
    name: 'ByteLite',
    href: '/technologies/bytelite',
    role: 'Finds and preserves structure in digital information.',
    detail:
      'It looks for deterministic structure in a file while keeping the ability to rebuild that file exactly, byte for byte. If it cannot rebuild the original exactly, it has failed, and that is a test with a yes-or-no answer.',
  },
  {
    name: 'ByteSight',
    href: '/technologies/bytesight',
    role: 'Investigates explicit structure in pictures.',
    detail:
      'It breaks an image into regions and records how they touch, contain, and border one another - and it does that the same way every time, so two runs on the same photo produce the same answer.',
  },
  {
    name: 'Deep Kore',
    href: '/technologies/deep-kore',
    role: 'Reasons over explicit relationships and governed concepts.',
    detail:
      'It works from stated relationships rather than from a guess about what usually comes next, and every answer it produces has to pass a governance check before anyone sees it.',
  },
  {
    name: '.root',
    href: RESEARCH_ROUTES.thesis,
    role: 'The structural-artifact research direction.',
    detail:
      'A self-contained file format we are researching, meant to hold enough structure to rebuild the original exactly. The long-term question is whether a reasoning system could one day read the structure in it directly.',
  },
];
---

<Layout
  noindex={true}
  title="What ByteLite LLC Is Building, in Plain English"
  description="A nontechnical explanation of ByteLite, ByteSight, Deep Kore, .root structural artifacts, and ByteLite LLC's long-term deterministic cognition research."
  keywords="ByteLite LLC plain English, what is ByteLite, what is Deep Kore, what is ByteSight, deterministic structure explained"
  canonical={`https://www.thebytelite.com${RESEARCH_ROUTES.plainEnglish}`}
>
  <Header slot="header" />
  <section class="section-sm"><div class="container"><Breadcrumbs items={[{ label: 'Research', href: RESEARCH_ROUTES.landing }, { label: 'Plain English' }]} /></div></section>
  <ProductSubNav items={SUBNAV} />

  <section class="pe-hero">
    <div class="container pe-container">
      <p class="eyebrow">Research, without the jargon</p>
      <h1>What We're Building, in Plain English</h1>
      <p class="pe-lede">
        {COMPANY_NAME}'s research explained without requiring a background in AI, compression, or
        computer science.
      </p>
      <p class="pe-meta">A 7-to-10 minute read. No prior knowledge assumed.</p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="section">
    <div class="container pe-container">
      <article class="pe-article">
        <h2>The short version</h2>
        <p>
          We are trying to find out whether a computer can describe something <em>exactly</em>, in
          fewer pieces, and then use that shorter description to think about it more cheaply.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is the whole idea. Everything below is what it means in practice, why it might
          matter, and what we do not yet know.
        </p>

        <h2>Start with a smaller example</h2>
        <p>
          Suppose someone asks you to add 7 to itself six times. You could write it out:
          7 + 7 + 7 + 7 + 7 + 7. That is six additions, and it works.
        </p>
        <p>
          Or you could notice something. That entire chain of additions is the same as one
          multiplication: 7 &times; 6. You did not approximate. You did not round. You found a
          shorter rule that gives the identical answer, every time, for any two numbers.
        </p>
        <div class="pe-callout">
          <p>
            Repeated addition became multiplication. The answer did not change. The amount of work
            did.
          </p>
        </div>
        <p>
          Multiplication is not a trick or a shortcut that sometimes fails. It is a genuinely
          shorter description of something a longer procedure was doing all along, and somebody had
          to discover it before anyone could use it.
        </p>
        <p>
          Our research asks whether a computer can do that kind of discovery for itself - carefully,
          provably, and only when the shorter version really does give the identical answer.
        </p>

        <h2>The four names you will see on this site</h2>
        <p>
          {COMPANY_NAME} has several projects. They look like separate products, and in a sense
          they are, but they are all asking the same question in different places.
        </p>
        <div class="pe-pieces">
          {PIECES.map((piece) => (
            <div class="pe-piece">
              <h3><a href={piece.href}>{piece.name}</a></h3>
              <p class="pe-piece-role">{piece.role}</p>
              <p>{piece.detail}</p>
            </div>
          ))}
        </div>

        <h2>Why "exactly" matters so much to us</h2>
        <p>
          A lot of software is allowed to be approximately right. A photo filter that is slightly
          off still looks fine. A recommendation that is a bit wrong is just a bad suggestion.
        </p>
        <p>
          We deliberately work in the places where approximately right is not good enough - where
          you can hold up the original and the rebuilt copy and check whether they are identical.
          That is a harsh standard, and that is the point. It means we cannot talk our way out of a
          failure. Either the file comes back byte for byte, or it does not.
        </p>
        <p>
          Working to that standard first is also how we avoid fooling ourselves later. If a system
          cannot preserve something as simple as a file, we should not trust it to preserve
          something as complicated as what a person meant.
        </p>

        <h2>The teacher and the student</h2>
        <p>
          Here is the part that ties the projects together, and it is the most speculative part of
          this page.
        </p>
        <p>
          ByteLite can already do a long, careful, step-by-step deterministic process. Think of it
          as the teacher: slow, thorough, and correct.
        </p>
        <p>
          Deep Kore is the student. The research question is whether the student can watch enough
          of that long process to work out a shorter rule that produces the same result - the way
          somebody watching enough repeated addition eventually works out multiplication.
        </p>
        <p>
          But the student is not allowed to just <em>guess</em> a shorter rule and start using it.
          Before a shorter rule replaces a longer one, it has to be shown to give the same answer,
          to whatever standard the situation demands. A shortcut for arranging a picture and a
          shortcut for something safety-related do not get the same amount of scrutiny, and neither
          borrows the other's.
        </p>
        <p>
          If the shorter rule cannot be shown to be equivalent, it does not get used. It gets
          written down as a candidate that did not qualify, and we keep the long way.
        </p>

        <h2>Knowing something is not the same as being allowed to do it</h2>
        <p>
          This is the part of the work that has nothing to do with cleverness, and it is the part
          we would defend hardest.
        </p>
        <p>
          A system can work something out and still have no business acting on it. It might be
          missing a fact it does not know it is missing. It might not have anyone's permission. It
          might be about to do something nobody can undo. Working out an answer does not settle any
          of that.
        </p>
        <p>
          So in our systems, the part that reasons is not the part that decides what may be said or
          done. Every answer has to pass a separate check first, and that check can refuse. There
          is no path around it &mdash; not a policy we follow, but a rule the code enforces and a
          test verifies.
        </p>
        <p>
          The other half of that is admitting what was never known. If the system did not observe
          something, it has to carry that gap forward as a gap, rather than quietly filling it in
          with a guess that later gets read as a fact. Stopping and saying "I can't answer that
          yet" is, in this architecture, a correct outcome rather than a failure.
        </p>

        <h2>Four words that get mixed up constantly</h2>
        <p>These sound similar and mean different things. The distinction matters for the rest of this page.</p>
        <dl class="pe-glossary">
          <div class="pe-glossary-row">
            <dt>Compression</dt>
            <dd>asks: what in here does not need to be repeated?</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="pe-glossary-row">
            <dt>Understanding</dt>
            <dd>asks: do all these repeated observations follow from one rule?</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="pe-glossary-row">
            <dt>Prediction</dt>
            <dd>applies a rule forward, to something you have not seen yet.</dd>
          </div>
          <div class="pe-glossary-row">
            <dt>Simulation</dt>
            <dd>applies predictive rules over and over, to see where things go.</dd>
          </div>
        </dl>
        <p>
          They form a ladder, and each rung is much harder than the one below it. We have real,
          tested work at the bottom of that ladder. The top of it is a research goal we have not
          reached and may not reach.
        </p>

        <h2>What is real today</h2>
        <p>
          This is the part most companies blur, so here it is directly. Today, in tested form,
          there is:
        </p>
        <ul class="pe-list pe-list-yes">
          <li>a system that can rebuild certain files exactly and count honestly what that cost;</li>
          <li>a system that can break an image into regions the same way twice and let a person correct it by hand;</li>
          <li>a reasoning system that classifies what kind of statement it is looking at and refuses to emit an answer that has not passed a governance check;</li>
          <li>a governance layer that every answer must pass through, with no way around it.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>And today, there is <strong>not</strong>:</p>
        <ul class="pe-list pe-list-no">
          <li>a product you can buy that compresses your files;</li>
          <li>an AI that understands anything, in the way a person would mean that word;</li>
          <li>a vision system that reliably recognizes objects - we have a documented failure on close-up portrait photos right now;</li>
          <li>anything that predicts or simulates the real world;</li>
          <li>anything verified by an outside party. All of the testing above is our own.</li>
        </ul>
        <p>
          The exact, per-project version of that list lives on
          <a href="/progress">Current Status</a> and <a href="/progress/validation-evidence">Validation Evidence</a>,
          including the failures. We publish those on purpose.
        </p>

        <h2>What we are hoping for, clearly labeled as hoping</h2>
        <p>
          Everything in this section is a <strong>goal</strong>. None of it exists. We are stating
          it because a research company that will not say what it is aiming at is not being
          straight with you either.
        </p>
        <ul class="pe-list pe-list-goal">
          <li>That the shorter rules a system discovers can be checked well enough to trust.</li>
          <li>That the same idea works on pictures as on files, and on reasoning as on pictures.</li>
          <li>That describing something in fewer pieces makes it cheaper to think about, so a fixed amount of computing goes further.</li>
          <li>That a system built this way can always show its work - what it saw, what it concluded, what it was not allowed to do, and what it never knew.</li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Why we bother saying what we cannot do</h2>
        <p>
          There is a lot of noise in this field. Plenty of claims sound impressive and cannot be
          checked by anybody outside the company making them.
        </p>
        <p>
          We would rather be the boring option: say what has been tested, say what has not, keep
          the failures on the site, and let the work be judged on that. It makes the company harder
          to attack, and it makes the research easier to believe when something does work.
        </p>

        <h2>What we keep private, and why</h2>
        <p>
          We explain what we are trying to do, why the pieces exist, and how we decide what to
          claim. We do not publish how the systems work internally - the specific methods, formats,
          and procedures that make them do what they do.
        </p>
        <p>
          That is not evasiveness about results. Every result on this site is stated with its
          evidence and its limits. It is that a one-person company's research methods are the thing
          it actually has.
        </p>

        <div class="pe-next">
          <h2>Where to go next</h2>
          <div class="pe-next-row">
            <a href={RESEARCH_ROUTES.thesis} class="btn btn-primary">The full research thesis</a>
            <a href="/progress" class="btn btn-outline">What exists today</a>
            <a href="/architecture" class="btn btn-outline">How the pieces fit together</a>
          </div>
        </div>
      </article>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="section section-alt">
    <div class="container pe-container">
      <DisclosureBoundary variant="short" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <Footer slot="footer" />
</Layout>

<style>
.pe-container { max-width: 720px; }
.pe-hero { padding: 4rem 0 2.5rem; }
.pe-lede { font-size: 1.125rem; color: #a0a8c0; line-height: 1.75; margin-top: 1rem; }
.pe-meta { font-size: .875rem; color: #555e7a; margin-top: 1rem; }

.pe-article h2 {
  font-size: clamp(1.25rem, 2.4vw, 1.625rem);
  color: #e4e7f2;
  margin: 3rem 0 1rem;
  scroll-margin-top: 5.5rem;
}
.pe-article > h2:first-child { margin-top: 0; }
.pe-article p {
  font-size: 1.0625rem;
  color: #a0a8c0;
  line-height: 1.85;
  margin-bottom: 1.125rem;
}
.pe-article strong { color: #e4e7f2; font-weight: 600; }
.pe-article em { color: #c4c8f0; }
.pe-article a { color: #818cf8; }
.pe-article a:hover, .pe-article a:focus-visible { color: #a5b4fc; }

.pe-callout {
  margin: 1.75rem 0;
  padding: 1.25rem 1.5rem;
  border-left: 3px solid #6366f1;
  background: rgba(99,102,241,.05);
  border-radius: 0 .5rem .5rem 0;
}
.pe-callout p { margin: 0; color: #c4c8f0; font-size: 1.0625rem; }

.pe-pieces { display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: 1rem; margin: 1.5rem 0 1.75rem; }
.pe-piece {
  background: #0d1120; border: 1px solid #1a2235; border-left: 3px solid #6366f1;
  border-radius: .75rem; padding: 1.375rem 1.5rem;
}
.pe-piece h3 { font-size: 1.0625rem; margin-bottom: .25rem; }
.pe-piece h3 a { color: #e4e7f2; }
.pe-piece h3 a:hover, .pe-piece h3 a:focus-visible { color: #818cf8; }
.pe-piece p { font-size: .9375rem; margin-bottom: 0; }
.pe-piece .pe-piece-role { color: #818cf8; font-weight: 500; margin-bottom: .5rem; }

.pe-glossary {
  margin: 1.5rem 0 1.75rem;
  background: #0d1120; border: 1px solid #1a2235; border-radius: .75rem;
  overflow: hidden;
}
.pe-glossary-row {
  display: grid; grid-template-columns: 9rem minmax(0, 1fr); gap: 1rem;
  padding: .875rem 1.5rem; border-bottom: 1px solid #1a2235;
}
.pe-glossary-row:last-child { border-bottom: none; }
@media (max-width: 560px) { .pe-glossary-row { grid-template-columns: 1fr; gap: .25rem; } }
.pe-glossary dt { font-size: .9375rem; font-weight: 600; color: #e4e7f2; }
.pe-glossary dd { font-size: .9375rem; color: #a0a8c0; line-height: 1.65; margin: 0; }

.pe-list { list-style: none; padding: 0; margin: 0 0 1.5rem; display: flex; flex-direction: column; gap: .625rem; }
.pe-list li { position: relative; padding-left: 1.875rem; font-size: 1rem; color: #a0a8c0; line-height: 1.75; }
.pe-list-yes li::before { content: "\2713"; position: absolute; left: .25rem; top: 0; color: #34d399; font-weight: 700; }
.pe-list-no li::before { content: "\00D7"; position: absolute; left: .3rem; top: 0; color: #f87171; font-weight: 700; }
.pe-list-goal li::before { content: "\2192"; position: absolute; left: .25rem; top: 0; color: #a78bfa; font-weight: 700; }

.pe-next { margin-top: 3rem; padding-top: 2rem; border-top: 1px solid #1a2235; }
.pe-next h2 { margin-top: 0; }
.pe-next-row { display: flex; gap: .75rem; flex-wrap: wrap; }
</style>
